      "content": "thanks. u cut off at the end there though lol. can u finish that last point about visual design and maybe give me some tips on how to find those \"hidden\" accessibility issues that aren't obvious during a first pass? like the kind of stuff that usually gets missed in testing.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, I'll complete that point for you and provide some tips on uncovering hidden accessibility issues.\n\n5. **Visual Design:**\n   - Ensure consistent and predictable navigation - use clearly defined patterns for menus, action buttons, etc.\n   - Minimize the use of color for important informational purposes to not rely on color alone because some users might not perceive it.\n   - Ensure adequate spacing so that important elements stand out and are easily distinguishable.\n   - Provide focus indicators for visual confirmation that elements are being interacted with.\n   - Ensure that all visual content has text alternatives.\n\nTo find hidden accessibility issues:\n\n1. **Edge-to-Edge Testing:**\n   - Test the app on different devices with varying screen sizes and resolutions to ensure elements aren’t cut off.\n\n2. **Interactive Testing:**\n   - Use the app in real-life scenarios - simulate user actions for students like swiping, tapping, pinching.\n\n3. **Screen Reader Testing:**\n   - Go beyond screen reader screening. Use multiple screen readers to see how your app performs with each one, as they might interpret content differently.\n\n4. **Color Contrast Checker:**\n   - Use multiple color contrast checkers to detect color combinations that might look different to users.\n\n5.",
      "source": "target",
